/T (Lack of robust multi-class flood segmentation: The majority of flood detection techniques currently in use perform binary water/no-water segmentation using a single technique without benchmarking across multiple approaches \(classical, foundation models like SAM, transformer-based segmenters\) or differentiating between flood water, debris, and damaged structures. This results in coarse, unreliable masks that don't capture the full picture required for damage assessment.)
/E (Lack of robust multi-class flood segmentation: The majority of flood detection techniques currently in use perform binary water/no-water segmentation using a single technique without benchmarking across multiple approaches \(classical, foundation models like SAM, transformer-based segmenters\) or differentiating between flood water, debris, and damaged structures. This results in coarse, unreliable masks that don't capture the full picture required for damage assessment.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
257 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 693 0 R
/T (Lack of proper report generation: Current automated systems either use standalone LLMs to generate situational reports without connecting claims to verifiable image-derived features or authoritative sources, or they output raw segmentation masks without any interpretation. This results in reports that are either unusable by responders or prone to hallucinated, untraceable severity/impact assessments.)
/E (Lack of proper report generation: Current automated systems either use standalone LLMs to generate situational reports without connecting claims to verifiable image-derived features or authoritative sources, or they output raw segmentation masks without any interpretation. This results in reports that are either unusable by responders or prone to hallucinated, untraceable severity/impact assessments.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [2]
>>
